<title>DENMARK: Danish April North Sea oil production down 1.9 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish April North Sea oil production down 1.9 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-05-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) oil production from its North Sea fields fell 1.9 percent in April 1997 from March, operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>It told Reuters that oil production was 914,000 tonnes in April, or 223,358 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 227,742 bpd in March.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third-biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain.</p>
<p>Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p>Field			April 97	  March 97	April 96</p>
<p>Dan			   66.5		67.8	   64.4</p>
<p>Kraka			  6.1		 6.2	    6.0</p>
<p>Regnar			 0.0		 0.0	    0.8</p>
<p>Gorm			  50.5		49.3	   47.9</p>
<p>Skjold			33.6		34.8	   35.9</p>
<p>Rolf			   4.2		 4.3	    3.8</p>
<p>Dagmar			 0.3		 0.3	    0.4</p>
<p>Tyra			  23.3		26.9	   27.2</p>
<p>Valdemar		     3.1		 2.6	    3.8</p>
<p>Roar			   7.3		 8.5	    7.1</p>
<p>Svend			 25.1		27.0	     --</p>
<p>Harald			 3.4		 0.0	     --</p>
<p>Total			223.4	     227.7	   197.5</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell group and Texaco Inc own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>--Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom, +45-33-969652</p>
